WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.597
Project B focuses on time grain.

00:00:02.597 --> 00:00:06.047
The source table has one row per order event.

00:00:06.047 --> 00:00:10.557
We truncate the timestamp to month, then group at that new grain.

00:00:10.557 --> 00:00:17.817
The output contains monthly order count, distinct customers, revenue, and average order value.

00:00:17.817 --> 00:00:23.352
This is the kind of feature table commonly used for dashboards or later modeling.

00:00:23.352 --> 00:00:29.397
Connect this idea to an operations team aligning timestamps across locations.

00:00:29.397 --> 00:00:35.142
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:35.142 --> 00:00:42.667
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:42.667 --> 00:00:48.217
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:48.217 --> 00:00:54.000
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
